steps.body:
A **class** is a blueprint for your own type. It lists **fields**, the data every object of that type carries. Declare it after `Main` with no `public`. In a real project each public class lives in its own file named after it, and in this one-file setup that public class is `Main`.

```java
class Dog {
    String name;
    int age;
}
```

`new Dog()` creates an **object** (an instance) from the blueprint. Read and write its fields with a dot:

```java
Dog rex = new Dog();
rex.name = "Rex";
rex.age = 3;
System.out.println(rex.name); // Rex
```

Each object has its own copy of every field. A field you never set starts at a default value, just like an array slot: `0` for whole numbers, `0.0` for doubles, `false` for booleans, and `null` for references such as `String`. Local variables get no default, which is why reading one before assigning it is a compile error.

You may also see a class nested inside `Main` as `static class Dog`. Without `static`, `main` cannot create it, and `new Dog()` is a compile error. This course keeps classes after `Main`.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog rex = new Dog();
        System.out.println("Before: " + rex.name + ", " + rex.age);
        rex.name = "Rex";
        rex.age = 3;

        Dog bella = new Dog();
        bella.name = "Bella";
        bella.age = 5;

        System.out.printf("%s is %d years old%n", rex.name, rex.age);
        System.out.printf("%s is %d years old%n", bella.name, bella.age);
        System.out.println("Total age: " + (rex.age + bella.age));
    }
}

class Dog {
    String name;
    int age;
}

