steps.body:
Setting every field by hand is tedious and easy to forget. A **constructor** runs when you call `new` and sets the object up in one line. It has the class's name and no return type, not even `void`:

```java
class Rectangle {
    int width;
    int height;

    Rectangle(int width, int height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }
}

Rectangle r = new Rectangle(3, 4);
```

The parameters share the fields' names, so inside the constructor plain `width` means the parameter. `this` is the object being built, so `this.width` is its field. Forget `this` and `width = width;` copies the parameter onto itself, leaving the field at `0`. Add `void` by mistake and it becomes an ordinary method, so `new Rectangle(3, 4)` stops compiling.

A class with no constructor gets an invisible no-argument one, which is why `new Dog()` worked. Once you write any constructor, that free one disappears: `new Rectangle()` is now a compile error unless you also write a `Rectangle()` constructor yourself.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Rectangle small = new Rectangle(3, 4);
        Rectangle wide = new Rectangle(5, 2);
        Rectangle square = new Rectangle(6, 6);

        System.out.printf("%d x %d, area %d%n", small.width, small.height, small.width * small.height);
        System.out.printf("%d x %d, area %d%n", wide.width, wide.height, wide.width * wide.height);
        System.out.printf("%d x %d, area %d%n", square.width, square.height, square.width * square.height);
        int total = small.width * small.height + wide.width * wide.height + square.width * square.height;
        System.out.println("Total area: " + total);
    }
}

class Rectangle {
    int width;
    int height;

    Rectangle(int width, int height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }
}

